      "message": "I guess, I still don\u0027t get why it\u0027s worth going through all the trouble of adding a SparseIntArray when the entire code could just be:\n```\nprivate boolean isFirewallAllowlist(int chain) {\n    switch (chain) {\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_DOZABLE:\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_POWERSAVE:\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_RESTRICTED:\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_LOW_POWER_STANDBY:\n            return true;\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_STANDBY:\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_OEM_DENY_1:\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_OEM_DENY_2:\n        case FIREWALL_CHAIN_OEM_DENY_3:\n            return false;\n        default:\n            throw new ServiceSpecificException(EINVAL, \"Invalid firewall chain\");\n    }\n}\n```\nWhile the difference is probably not large, this switch/case should generate a single tableswitch instruction in bytecode, while the SparseIntArray performs a binary search. Plus, there is no static memory allocation. For the few entries here, that is not a big difference either -- but given that the switch/case is also less code and encapsulates everything in one function, I strongly prefer it over the SparseIntArray approach.",
